.TH quot "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
quot \- summarize file system usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBquot [\-c] [\-f] [\-n] [\-t] \fIfilesystem ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
quot -- summarize file system usage
Usage:	quot [ -c ] [ -f ] [ -n ] [ -t ] filesystem ...
Normally prints number of blocks used in filesystem by each user.
Options:
	-c	Print file size, number of files of size,
		and cumulative total blocks up to size
	-f	Print number of files in addition to number of blocks per user
	-n	Input (i-number, file system) pairs one per line;
		output owners and file names
		(e.g.: ncheck fs | sort +0n | quot -n fs)
		Works only on small raw devices
	-t	Print totals (where applicable)
The options '-c', '-n' and other options are disjoint.
.HE
.B quot
scans each named
.I filesystem
(a block or raw special file, or a mounted directory) and reports on
how its space is used.
With no options,
.B quot
prints, for each user owning any file in the file system, the number
of blocks that user occupies, sorted with the largest space user
first, and the owner's login name (or numeric uid, if not found in
.BR /etc/passwd ).
If more than one
.I filesystem
is given,
.B quot
prints the name of each before its report.
.PP
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Instead of a per-user report, print a three-column breakdown by file
size: file size in blocks, the number of files of that size, and the
cumulative total of blocks used by files of that size or smaller.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Add a column giving the number of files, as well as the number of
blocks, for each user.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Read, on the standard input, lines of i-number and file name pairs
already sorted by i-number -- typically the output of
.B ncheck
on the same
.IR filesystem ,
piped through
.BR "sort +0n" ,
as in
.BR "ncheck fs | sort +0n | quot \-n fs" .
For each i-number in
.I filesystem
that matches one in the input,
.B quot
prints the owner and the file name.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Add a totals line to the per-user or size-breakdown report.
.PP
.BR \-c ", " \-f ", and " \-n
are mutually exclusive; giving more than one is an error.
.B \-t
may be combined with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-f ,
but not with
.BR \-n .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/passwd\fR	to translate user ids into names
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ac ,
.BR dcheck ,
.BR ncheck ,
.B sort
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B quot
complains and skips a
.I filesystem
it cannot open or whose superblock size fields are unreasonable.
.SH NOTES
.B quot
reads the raw device directly; it runs much faster given a raw
special file for
.I filesystem
than a block special file, and needs read permission on that device,
which is ordinarily restricted to the superuser.
